import type { GeospatialCamera, GeospatialLimits } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function useModeLimits(camera: GeospatialCamera, limits: GeospatialLimits): void {
    const l = camera.limits;
    l.planetRadius = limits.planetRadius;
    l.radiusMin = limits.radiusMin;
    l.radiusMax = limits.radiusMax;
    l.pitchMin = limits.pitchMin;
    l.pitchMax = limits.pitchMax;
    l.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale;
    l.yawMin = limits.yawMin;
    l.yawMax = limits.yawMax;
}
```

This mutates the camera's existing limits object in place, which per note 39 means the new values will be used by the camera's clamps on the next yaw/pitch/radius write that changes the value.

Since the app never changes a mode's limits object after creating it, we don't need to worry about the passed `limits` being mutated later.

The requirement says "whatever zoom (radius) or heading (yaw) the app writes to the camera must be kept inside that mode's zoom range and heading range" - this is handled by the camera's built-in clamping mechanism, which uses `camera.limits` fields. By mutating those fields in place, we ensure the clamps use the correct values.

And "camera.limits must report that mode's values" - since we're mutating the same object that `camera.limits` points to, it will report the correct values.

"Switching to another mode later must give that mode's ranges instead" - calling `useModeLimits` again with a different limits object will mutate the fields again.

